WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.420
 This is the Heartland Daily Podcast.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.640
 Hello and welcome to the Heartland Institute's Daily Podcast. I'm Sterling Burnett,

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.640
 Heartland Senior Fellow and Managing Editor of Environment and Climate News.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.240
 Recently, Dr. Ronan Connolly co-authored a paper with 22 other scientists from 14 different countries

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:38.280
 that was published in a peer-reviewed journal, Research in Astronomy and Astrophysics,

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:43.340
 in which they attempted to ascertain the impact of solar activity on climate change.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:48.500
 To do so, they undertook a comprehensive analysis of the 16 published solar data sets.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.720
 They came to the conclusion that natural factors, driven in part by solar activity,

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.680
 accounted for most, if not all, climate change the Earth has experienced recently.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.140
 Wow, seems like good news.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.220
 Were they hailed as heroes for delivering this message?

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:07.660
 One which frees the world from the pain that would come from premature cessation of fossil fuel use?

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.020
 You know the answer to that. They weren't.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.900
 Instead, Facebook and other social media outlets have done their best to suppress the report

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:21.000
 and prevent the sharing and discussion of it by their members and their supposedly,

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:27.300
 because their independent fact checkers labeled a news article that covered the story,

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.200
 incorrect and misleading.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:34.260
 Here to discuss both the research and the social media backlash is Dr. Ronan Connolly,

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:35.400
 the lead author of the paper.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:37.080
 Ronan, thanks for being here.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:38.840
 Thank you for having me on.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:46.520
 Ronan, before we discuss your recent paper and the social media brouhaha that has erupted around it,

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:51.920
 for our listeners who may not be familiar with you or the Center for Environmental Research and Earth Sciences,

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.200
 where you work, please give us a little background on yourself, the Institute,

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.480
 and how you came to work on environment and climate issues.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:01:59.980
 Okay.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:10.760
 So, to try and keep things fairly short, if people are interested in learning a lot more about it,

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:19.040
 my personal website is ronancondlyscience.com, and that will give a background on my own background

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.340
 and also links to all of the papers, including the one we were discussing here.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:29.780
 So, I, but in a nutshell, I'm a scientist.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:37.860
 I, my degree was, a basic degree in 2000 was in chemistry and maths.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:42.320
 Then I did a PhD in computer modeling and polymer physics.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:49.400
 And then I started working with my father, Dr. Michael Connolly, who's also a co-author on this paper.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.300
 I still work with him in research capacity as well.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:59.760
 But I started working with him on the environment, developing sustainable methods of fish farming,

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:12.380
 wastewater treatment, and also trying to develop improved heat, energy storage,

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:19.340
 and energy transmission, and energy technologies.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:30.360
 And then, by 2009, I think it was, we were working in the environment.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:35.220
 We were environmentalists, but everybody kept on asking, oh, you're talking about environment.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.520
 So, you probably can tell us all about climate change.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:45.480
 And so, I, myself and my father, we started, I said, we actually should look at this.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:46.900
 This is a big issue.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:50.060
 And then we started digging in deep.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:04:03.000
 And we realized that there was a lot of problems with the underlying science on a lot of different aspects.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:11.640
 That when you hear people saying the science is settled on almost, just in general, as a rule of thumb,

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:19.540
 if somebody is saying the science is settled on something, then they're not talking about science.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:23.140
 Science doesn't work by democracy.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:32.700
 Science, if anything, is like a monarch or, you know, I actually can say mother nature.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.820
 Mother nature doesn't really care what we think it should be doing.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:44.280
 So, doing a straw poll of what we think mother nature is doing is not the same.

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:51.420
 Anyway, we started digging into what we know about the science on climate change.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:05:00.860
 And after about five years, we decided we'd publish all of our findings online in one go.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:08.400
 We set up a website, Open Peer Review Journal, where we put up eight of our papers that we had written up.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:19.320
 And we just went directly to the public, to the internet community and said, can you review our work, the entire scientific community?

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:34.060
 And then we also set up a blog and then we started engaging with other scientists and all doing this in our own spare time.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:47.940
 And then 2018, about 2008, a few years ago, myself and one of the colleagues that we started collaborating with over the last few years,

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:52.940
 Dr. Willie Soon, at the Harvard-Switsonian Center for Astrophysics,

00:05:53.340 --> 00:06:03.120
 we were kind of saying, can we actually get some work done, solid work done on climate change?

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:10.820
 Because Willie was working with it in a, you know, working in the, he's working for the Center for Astrophysics.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:16.340
 So, he's working in astrophysics research, but he's also very interested in climate change.

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:20.300
 I was working in my spare time on this, so was my father.

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:27.760
 And then we said, can we actually start doing work on climate change?

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:28.980
 For full time.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:30.080
 Yes.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:37.600
 And so, we were looking, and a big problem that we've been finding is that if you actually, at the moment,

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:45.960
 most research is funded by, certainly in Europe, it's almost entirely funded by governments.

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:52.600
 Once you apply for a grant, you do have some industry funding in, particularly in the U.S.,

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:55.860
 and you can have, like, TIC-TAC fundings as well.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:04.980
 But the main research is, all of them, you apply for a grant about what you are going to do for the next three years.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.000
 And it kind of is very hindered.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.200
 If you know my problem is...

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:12.820
 Wait, wait, wait, wait.

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:16.140
 We're going to be getting a little far field into this.

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:17.080
 Okay, okay, okay.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:19.960
 Tell us about how you founded the Center for Environmental...

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:26.860
 So, the bottom line is, we set up this, an institution called Center for Environmental Research and Earth Sciences,

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:31.180
 and the website is series-science.com.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:41.540
 And what we do is, we get our funding from people that donate, that want us to do science.

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:49.280
 But the donors and contributors, they can't influence what we will do or find.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:55.220
 They can't influence our research direction, our conclusions in any way.

00:07:55.220 --> 00:08:04.160
 All that they're doing is, if they want to see good science being done, then they can contribute to our group.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:09.200
 And then we do, we follow the science where it takes us.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:11.900
 So, we don't know where it will take us.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:16.700
 But we don't have a fixed outcome.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:26.540
 Whereas, the standard approach to doing it is, you decide, this is what you're going to find in the next three years, roughly.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:36.660
 I'm simplifying things, but it doesn't, we think that that doesn't really lead to proper, deep scientific research.

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:46.860
 So, if any of your listeners like what we're doing, if you go to series-science.com, you can donate.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:54.840
 We're a very small group at the moment, but the more people to donate, we can try expanding it.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:09:00.240
 Yeah, I noticed both your father and your mother have PhDs.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:05.080
 So, you come from quite a background where people really value education.

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:10.680
 And I'm wondering, you know, you mentioned, I saw that you'd done stuff on fisheries, and I didn't look it up.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:22.560
 But I think we were actually in the same collection from a few years ago on fisheries problems, published by, I don't think it was the Adam Smith Institute.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:30.840
 It was published by the Institute for Economic – it's an English think tank.

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:35.100
 And they published a book called Sea Change.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:38.560
 And I think you have an article in it, and I have an article in it.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.840
 I don't think I do, but –

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:43.420
 Okay, well, I could be wrong about that.

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:43.840
 I could be wrong about that.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:44.200
 Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:44.880
 But –

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:45.720
 I've seen an M4.

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:47.720
 Yeah, definitely.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:51.520
 It's like – and by the way, so I'm working on this.

00:09:52.240 --> 00:10:04.040
 And also, Michael is still full-time working on the aquaculture research as well as part-time working with me and vice versa.

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:08.760
 So I'm working part-time with him, and he's working part-time with me on the aquaculture.

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:11.000
 Well, the fisheries – you know, fisheries are a big thing.

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:16.640
 It's something I've been interested in for a long time and have written about – I won't say extensively, but quite a bit.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:17.540
 Yeah, yeah.

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:21.320
 Like yourself, more and more of my time is dominated by climate issues.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:24.460
 But that's not really where my heart lies.

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:30.260
 So, Ronan, what was – you sort of described how you all decided to follow the science.

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:32.380
 So what was the impetus for your recent paper?

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:33.920
 I know you work with a long-time friend.

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:35.080
 You've already mentioned Willie Soon.

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:37.540
 We've worked with him at Heartland for a long time.

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:42.860
 How did the collaboration come together, and what was the impetus for this particular paper?

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:53.480
 Well, we've been working, as I say, we've been working with Willie Soon for a number of years now, and he's part of this series, Science.

00:10:55.080 --> 00:11:10.680
 The first paper that we did with Willie was in 2015, and this was a paper on looking at the relationship between solar variability and temperatures,

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:13.580
 which is something similar to this new one.

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:17.340
 So we published that in Earth Science Reviews.

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:25.840
 It's quite a – you know, it's quite a detailed review, quite very well received.

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:35.600
 But the – that was like six years ago, and we were also focusing on just a few issues.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:44.080
 So we felt that it was time to update and expand what we were doing, what we had done with that particular paper,

00:11:44.760 --> 00:12:03.800
 particularly as we were trying to look at – in that 2015 paper, we had looked very specifically at the role of what's called total solar irradiance.

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:13.580
 So this is the amount of energy that reaches the Earth from the sun, and it's TSI for short.

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:29.980
 So you have this – since the late 70s, early 80s, they've been directly monitoring the amount of sun – of total solar irradiance, TSI,

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:32.900
 that's reaching the Earth, TSI that's reaching the Earth using satellites.

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:39.940
 Before that, you have to use what are called solar proxies.

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:52.460
 So you need to use things like sunspot numbers, the ratios between the faculae, the plage areas.

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:59.080
 You have different aspects of solar activity, geomagnetic –

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:00.880
 Different types of proxy data.

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:01.500
 Yes.

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:06.680
 And there's a whole range of them that people can use.

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:15.200
 And then they can get them, and they can join them together, and then they can calibrate them with the satellite records.

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:27.480
 And then you have an estimates of TSI, of solar activity, that can go back hundreds of years, or in some of the cases, can go back thousands of years.

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:40.800
 And we were concerned that the UN Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, IPCC, in their fifth assessment report,

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:48.480
 they were arguing that they were getting all of their modelers, the modeling groups that were contributing to the report,

00:13:48.480 --> 00:14:00.280
 they were telling them to use a very specific range of these long-term solar estimates, of estimates of TSI.

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:03.480
 And we noticed that –

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:10.620
 So, just for me to put that in context, so the IPCC reports are supposed to be –

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:14.560
 they tout themselves as being a comprehensive survey of the literature.

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:20.960
 Well, I don't know that they used the word comprehensive, butch, anyway.

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:24.920
 Well, they say this the most thorough.

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:28.960
 They've touted it as, we look at more research.

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:38.520
 But here you're saying the people at the top told the researchers, the executives, we're telling you which sets of data to use.

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:49.200
 They were strongly recommending the groups that were organizing the CMIP 5 project, and then now the more recent one is the CMIP 6.

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:56.300
 This is for the latest report that was just coincidentally published online at the same time as our paper.

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:10.780
 But the day for the fifth assessment report, they had been strongly encouraging the modeling groups, these are the ones that you should use.

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:11.260
 Right.

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:22.620
 And the modeling groups, in fairness, they are – they're just asking, which ones should we plug into our models?

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:24.720
 You know, what's the best one?

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:28.520
 And so they're not necessarily experts on solar activity.

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:30.960
 They just know we need to put in a data set.

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:32.300
 Which one do we include?

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:34.700
 So they're looking up at it.

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:46.060
 So it turns out that all – for the fifth assessment report, all four of the estimates that were used were what we call low variability estimates.

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:51.740
 And so these are ones that – two things to note about them.

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:56.320
 All of them show a decline in solar activity since the 60s.

00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:04.780
 And all of them show very little solar variability overall, long term.

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:24.020
 And so if the climate models, when they are doing what's called the attribution, they have – this is – I'm getting technical, but I hope, you know, you can – I hope your audience say is – you need this sort of nuance.

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:26.100
 We understand what attribution is here.

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:39.620
 So the IPCC, when you hear their statement, we are extremely – it's unequivocal that the warming is human-caused, that past warming is human-caused.

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:55.240
 The main source for their claim is that they run the computer models using solar data sets, a TSI data set, and a volcanic data set.

00:16:55.300 --> 00:17:03.200
 A volcanic data set shows that after large volcanic eruptions, there's a short temporary cooling of a couple of years.

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:14.940
 It can't explain warming, so if you want to explain warming with the current models, it has – from a natural factors, it needs to be due to increasing solar activity.

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:21.520
 And so they – all of the models were told, these are the range that are acceptable.

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:33.220
 They all used one of four – for the fifth assessment report – one of four solar data sets, all of which showed a decline in the solar activity since the 60s.

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:53.680
 If you have a decline in solar activity since the 60s, and that's the only way that your model shows of explaining global warming, well, then they're saying, then we know for sure that any of the warming since the 1960s –

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:55.940
 Because the sun can't be driving it because there's no –

00:17:55.940 --> 00:18:04.200
 Yeah, and that's the only natural factor that they consider for causing a multidecadal warming.

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:24.620
 So this is how they can say unequivocally or extremely likely, all of these things, because none of the models, when they plug in one of these four TSI data sets, will show any warming, natural warming.

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:31.540
 But what did your – what questions did you attempt to answer, and what did you find in opposition?

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:49.420
 Well, yeah, so what we were prompted on this is that we actually saw that we were very concerned that even though the – our 2015 paper – this is – we've shown that in the 2015 paper.

00:18:49.420 --> 00:19:19.020
 And even though our paper has been cited favorably by one of the co-chairs of the IPCC AR6, ironically enough, the CMIP6 new project for the sixth assessment report was saying, we're going to now use – instead of having four, here's the new one that you should all use, all of the modeling groups, which is by – it's called Mattis et al.

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:20.580
 Until 2017.

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:23.300
 So it's another data set.

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:31.980
 And again, it shows the same thing, very – almost flat solar variability, but it has been declining since the late 50s.

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:47.680
 And so we're like, if you use that, you'll get the same answer, but we shouldn't – we don't know that, because we looked and we found – we found 16 different estimates of TSI.

00:19:48.620 --> 00:19:53.180
 And we showed – we postulated another four.

00:19:53.180 --> 00:20:00.960
 We just showed – we create another four that you could do that are equally plausible, you know, that we showed.

00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:03.780
 There's a load of different estimates you could do.

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:18.160
 And if you're just using all of the ones that will just show the same decline since the 50s, you're not actually doing a comprehensive scientific review.

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:33.300
 Another issue that we had – I don't want to get too long-winded on this, but there is a second issue – is that the – there is a – some of your listeners may be familiar with what's called the urban heat island problem.

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:47.420
 So when people are calculating global temperatures, they're using – the main data set that they use for calculating the land component is to use weather stations taken from all around the world.

00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:53.240
 Unfortunately, a large number of these stations –

00:20:53.240 --> 00:20:53.860
 They're compromised.

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:56.940
 Yeah, they happen to be in urban areas.

00:20:57.540 --> 00:21:09.100
 And they weren't – those urban areas – urban areas are warmer than the surrounding countryside because of the local – a locally effect from the building up of buildings.

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:20.480
 We won't get into the building up of buildings, but physically, you can actually measure as you go into a city, the temperature goes up, and when you leave the city, it cools down again.

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:24.600
 It's a very real phenomenon that can be found.

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:26.980
 And this grows over time.

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:49.540
 So there is a concern within the scientific community that if you are using urban records for calculating global temperatures, that you might be finding this local urban warming and mixing it up with the actual regional climate variability.

00:21:49.540 --> 00:22:02.800
 Now, the UNIPCC, they are convinced, and in their latest report, they said that this effect is less than 10% of the warming.

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:09.240
 They say this with very – they're very confident about this.

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:19.900
 But we were finding in our 2015 paper, we found actually just with our preliminary analysis, we found huge differences.

00:22:20.140 --> 00:22:29.600
 We took rural stations and compared for the northern hemisphere, there wasn't enough for the southern hemisphere, which is a problem in itself.

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:33.760
 We just said, let's just focus on the regions where we have enough data.

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:36.860
 And when we found that, we found a very different result.

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:47.320
 It showed that it was – you had warming from 18 – the late 19th century up to the 40s, then cooling to the 70s, and then warming again.

00:22:47.320 --> 00:23:05.760
 And that the recent warming was similar to the warming in the 40s, which is definitely not the same as you get with the standard data sets, which just show an almost continuous rising and, if anything, an acceleration in the last few decades.

00:23:06.140 --> 00:23:06.240
 Yeah.

00:23:07.540 --> 00:23:11.240
 Like I said, we've talked about the urban halon effect quite a bit.

00:23:11.300 --> 00:23:11.740
 We understand.

00:23:12.180 --> 00:23:13.580
 I like to describe it this way.

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:31.960
 If you take bad data, which I'm talking about the data from the urban areas, and you mix it with good data, which is the uncompromised weather stations, it's like taking bottled water and mixing it with river water and thinking you're getting better water.

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:32.680
 You're not.

00:23:33.260 --> 00:23:34.940
 You're just destroying the good data.

00:23:35.900 --> 00:23:36.020
 Yeah.

00:23:36.380 --> 00:23:38.760
 So, I mean, so we understand that.

00:23:38.880 --> 00:23:38.980
 Yeah.

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:39.880
 Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:23:39.880 --> 00:23:51.680
 The analogy – a similar analogy I'll do is if you put strawberries and banana into a blender, you can get a nice smoothie, but it's neither strawberries nor bananas.

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:52.480
 Nor bananas, yeah.

00:23:52.780 --> 00:23:53.340
 Yeah, yeah.

00:23:53.340 --> 00:24:10.360
 So, before the last decade of the 20th century, it was commonly accepted scientifically that solar activity, you know, periodic alternating cycles of hyperactivity and quiescence, played a huge role in driving regional and global climate change.

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:13.420
 Little ice ages, climate optimums, they were all tied to solar activity.

00:24:13.420 --> 00:24:25.160
 But since governments formed the IPCC under the auspices of the UN and directed them to study the human causes of climate change, the sun's influence on climate has been downplayed.

00:24:26.660 --> 00:24:28.340
 That's what your research shows, right?

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:38.540
 Is that they – you took 16 data sets as opposed to the one in the AR6 and the four they used in the AR5, and you found something quite different.

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:39.700
 What did you find?

00:24:39.700 --> 00:25:00.860
 Yeah, well, what we basically found, to just simplify it, we showed that by choosing your temperature data sets and choosing your solar – by cherry-picking your data sets, your temperature data sets, and cherry-picking your solar data sets,

00:25:00.860 --> 00:25:09.240
 you could explain anything from 0% solar role to 100% solar role.

00:25:09.940 --> 00:25:26.160
 Now, as a scientist, when I see that, I'm like, this is – we're not – we shouldn't be deciding, saying, right, which – let's start honing in on a consensus.

00:25:26.620 --> 00:25:38.360
 This is the period when – when you're getting these results that are so different, you're not converging towards a consensus, you know, an actual consensus.

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:40.800
 So we need to –

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:46.420
 More importantly, you're not converging towards an understanding of what's really going on.

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:47.840
 I mean, that's what science is about.

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:48.700
 It's not about consensus.

00:25:48.700 --> 00:26:01.060
 You're not converging on the truth of the matter, and yet they're already pushing for policies based on something you haven't even decided scientifically.

00:26:01.720 --> 00:26:08.120
 Yeah, well, so, like, one of the quotes – there's a great quote that we give in the paper, for me, that sums it up.

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:09.580
 Why our approach?

00:26:09.580 --> 00:26:24.640
 So the IPCC – just to say it, because, you know, people can look it up for themselves – but the IPCC, they – they are trying to find a consensus

00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:32.480
 on all issues of scientific discussion relevant to climate change.

00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:34.260
 That's what they're doing.

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:41.700
 And initially, if you're not coming at it from a scientific background, that sounds like a good thing.

00:26:41.820 --> 00:26:47.040
 Oh, find the points, the consensus, find the consensus and all this, an expert consensus.

00:26:47.740 --> 00:26:56.020
 The problem is – and I just – just to give you this thing – is that science doesn't work that way.

00:26:56.020 --> 00:27:08.720
 If – if every – if all of the scientific evidence was clear and unequivocal on some particular issue, well, then the science would be done.

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:11.380
 Your job would be done.

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:15.240
 So then the scientists would say, right, we're done with that.

00:27:15.380 --> 00:27:17.120
 We won't go back at that.

00:27:17.240 --> 00:27:24.960
 We – you know, we don't have people going out and saying, I wonder – I wonder if gravity is still there.

00:27:25.320 --> 00:27:27.760
 Let's go outside and check that again.

00:27:27.940 --> 00:27:32.680
 Don't have – yeah, you don't have people studying – maybe we should find out where the earth is around.

00:27:33.220 --> 00:27:35.080
 Yeah, yeah, yeah, you don't have this.

00:27:35.080 --> 00:27:47.220
 So this is – when there is – science is actually – scientific research is about trying to dig into where the disagreements are.

00:27:47.220 --> 00:27:47.740
 And why.

00:27:47.740 --> 00:27:48.040
 And why.

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:49.740
 That's a large part of it.

00:27:49.740 --> 00:28:00.220
 I think Isaac Asimov, the great science fiction writer who passed away a few decades ago.

00:28:00.500 --> 00:28:07.780
 He was asked, you know, he was asked, you know, to describe scientific moments, discoveries.

00:28:07.780 --> 00:28:15.320
 And he said, like, I'm paraphrasing, but he said, people think that it's like a Eureka moment.

00:28:15.640 --> 00:28:28.500
 You know, the Archimedes thing, where the – Archimedes jumped – the story, he jumps out of his bat and he runs down the street naked saying, Eureka, Eureka, I've got it, I've got it.

00:28:28.500 --> 00:28:37.200
 But he said, actually, most scientific discoveries, the scientists will go and say, that's odd.

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:39.180
 That's it.

00:28:39.540 --> 00:28:43.260
 And say, this is something I wasn't expecting.

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:51.120
 So, you know, that's actually – scientific research is going against what we already know.

00:28:51.220 --> 00:28:54.820
 It's not the same as common sense or intuition.

00:28:54.820 --> 00:29:04.160
 In fact, if I were to just say that – to think about this, but it should make sense when you think about it.

00:29:04.520 --> 00:29:19.220
 I argue that all major scientific discoveries were non-intuitive because if it was intuitive, it wouldn't have been a major scientific discovery.

00:29:20.720 --> 00:29:22.560
 You wouldn't have needed science.

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:26.420
 And, you know, just as an aside, I'd say this.

00:29:27.080 --> 00:29:40.900
 If the science is truly settled, as they claim, right, the IPCC claims the science is settled, it's unequivocal, use whatever terminology you want to use, then that's great.

00:29:41.140 --> 00:29:42.300
 We can stop funding them.

00:29:43.280 --> 00:29:47.580
 No more funding for the IPCC because the science is settled.

00:29:47.960 --> 00:29:51.220
 Now we'll just move on and you guys shut up and we'll go do our business.

00:29:51.220 --> 00:29:52.620
 Yeah, yeah, your job is done.

00:29:52.860 --> 00:29:53.740
 Your job is done.

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:58.920
 The point is none of them embrace the back end of that.

00:29:59.480 --> 00:30:00.620
 They all embrace the front end.

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:01.360
 The science is settled.

00:30:01.820 --> 00:30:05.480
 But we need more money to continue the research.

00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:06.580
 Well, research into what?

00:30:06.680 --> 00:30:08.120
 You've already told us it's established.

00:30:08.920 --> 00:30:09.100
 Yeah.

00:30:09.740 --> 00:30:14.660
 So, like, I've just got to push back a little bit and defend my scientific colleagues.

00:30:14.660 --> 00:30:16.580
 That was a mistake in the beginning.

00:30:17.220 --> 00:30:28.140
 You know, the thing is, if you want to do science and the only way you can do science is by true granting and stuff granted, then you need to do it and then you can do it.

00:30:28.260 --> 00:30:32.720
 So, if you look at most of the grants on climate change, there are loads of grants.

00:30:32.720 --> 00:30:37.680
 But I would say that a lot of the research is dotting I's and crossing T's.

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:40.520
 That's the problem with that approach.

00:30:40.620 --> 00:30:48.240
 And that's what we're trying to avoid with CERI's science is we're saying we don't know where things will go.

00:30:48.240 --> 00:30:50.580
 It's not time to dot I's and cross T's.

00:30:50.660 --> 00:30:52.140
 We've got bigger questions to ask.

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:52.500
 I understand.

00:30:52.520 --> 00:30:52.980
 Yeah, yeah.

00:30:53.000 --> 00:31:00.020
 We're asking, are you sure that's the right word to be using in that sentence, let alone the spelling?

00:31:00.700 --> 00:31:09.880
 And the thing is, I just wanted to quote, I mentioned this quote in the paper, but it's from Sir Francis Bacon in 1605.

00:31:10.280 --> 00:31:26.760
 And he was just, he's one of the early, when science started to become a systematic approach, a philosophy, it was called natural philosophy at the time then.

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:34.140
 But he said that if we begin with certainties, we shall end in doubts.

00:31:34.640 --> 00:31:39.820
 But if we begin with doubts and are patient in them, we shall end in certainties.

00:31:40.800 --> 00:31:46.500
 Now, the IPCC began with certainties.

00:31:48.840 --> 00:31:49.760
 The UN...

00:31:49.760 --> 00:31:53.480
 The certainties where humans were causing climate change, find out how much bad.

00:31:53.800 --> 00:32:01.340
 Yeah, and in fact, you can look up, there's, you know, I would recommend reading Professor Roger Pelkey Jr.

00:32:02.020 --> 00:32:03.980
 has written some good stuff on this.

00:32:04.100 --> 00:32:07.480
 Like, he did a very important paper in 2005.

00:32:08.100 --> 00:32:11.620
 And then he's written books like Climate Fix and things like this.

00:32:11.620 --> 00:32:21.020
 He points out that the UN FCCC definition, so this is the UN Framework Convention on Climate Change,

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:24.580
 that it's the basis of all of these COP meetings.

00:32:24.740 --> 00:32:28.780
 Like, so we have this year, we're going to have the Glasgow annual...

00:32:28.780 --> 00:32:32.100
 The annual meeting is going to be in Glasgow this year in Scotland.

00:32:32.680 --> 00:32:39.200
 Every year since the 90s, the UN has been organising these meetings.

00:32:39.200 --> 00:32:52.780
 And the UN IPCC report is supposed to provide scientific support for the negotiations that have been taking place at these meetings.

00:32:53.100 --> 00:33:02.420
 But at these meetings, the definition of climate change that is used by the UN FCCC is...

00:33:02.420 --> 00:33:12.820
 I'm paraphrasing, you can look it up, but it essentially is any change in the climate that can be attributed to the human modification of the atmosphere.

00:33:14.100 --> 00:33:21.720
 So, the IPCC's definition, and my definition, would be any change in climate, right?

00:33:22.100 --> 00:33:24.160
 It's not that hard.

00:33:24.340 --> 00:33:27.520
 Climate change is a change in climate.

00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:41.880
 But at these COP meetings, COP meetings, the definition of climate change is any change in climate that can be attributed directly or indirectly to human modification of the atmosphere.

00:33:41.880 --> 00:33:55.880
 So, that was decided, this idea, the UNEP, the UNEP that is one of the co-founders of and one of the organisers of the IPCC.

00:33:55.880 --> 00:33:56.820
 Environment program, yeah.

00:33:56.980 --> 00:34:02.980
 Yeah, of the IPCC report, and also the organisers of the FCCC definition.

00:34:02.980 --> 00:34:19.480
 They had already decided before the first assessment report of the IPCC that human-caused climate change was an urgent catastrophe.

00:34:19.480 --> 00:34:47.480
 And it is a nice, even a nice interview from 1990 with the director of the UNEP saying that by the year 2000, huge parts of the world will be flooded and major cities will be flooded if we don't stop urgently climate change happening.

00:34:47.480 --> 00:34:54.560
 This is all decided before the first assessment report of the IPCC had even been started.

00:34:55.040 --> 00:35:05.780
 Yeah, so once you set the parameters, once you say, this is what we know is happening, now go out and show it's happening, you've got your marching orders.

00:35:06.480 --> 00:35:20.340
 Yeah, and the thing is that the IPCC as an organisation includes a lot of people and a lot of my colleagues have been involved in the IPCC reports, but it's a very hierarchical structure.

00:35:20.940 --> 00:35:30.480
 So, you can be a reviewer or anything like that, yeah, anything like this.

