 All right, back to the show.

00:36:35.240 --> 00:36:38.440
 Yeah, but let's get back to your story here.

00:36:38.980 --> 00:36:39.280
 Right.

00:36:39.460 --> 00:36:47.160
 Not just the details of the IPCC and its virtues and flaws, but you've got this paper.

00:36:47.420 --> 00:36:53.740
 And this paper indicates that based on the 16 data sets, as opposed to one or four that they were using,

00:36:54.300 --> 00:36:58.780
 it shows that solar activity could account for almost all of the present warming,

00:36:59.220 --> 00:37:06.920
 or solar activity combined with other natural factors, or almost none of it, because there's this huge range.

00:37:07.320 --> 00:37:07.420
 Yeah.

00:37:07.460 --> 00:37:10.720
 And that's what we need to examine, or that's what scientists should be examining.

00:37:11.220 --> 00:37:11.420
 Yeah.

00:37:11.420 --> 00:37:21.300
 And rather than that opening up a conversation and a discussion in the community, what happened was a news report came out in the e-book Times.

00:37:21.800 --> 00:37:22.020
 Yeah.

00:37:22.400 --> 00:37:28.660
 And I take it that they fairly represented what your study said.

00:37:29.020 --> 00:37:29.540
 Yeah.

00:37:29.740 --> 00:37:31.680
 And then social media got involved.

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:32.520
 Yeah.

00:37:32.720 --> 00:37:38.580
 And that's what the story has been about, is the social media has attempted to suppress.

00:37:39.160 --> 00:37:49.060
 They labeled the news story about your paper false and misleading, and thus they are suppressing or attempting to suppress discussion of your paper itself.

00:37:49.180 --> 00:37:50.500
 They're blocking the sharing of it.

00:37:51.020 --> 00:38:00.400
 So, how has that affected the ability to discuss your article, and how have your fellow researchers and yourself responded?

00:38:00.840 --> 00:38:01.040
 Yeah.

00:38:01.160 --> 00:38:01.420
 Okay.

00:38:01.540 --> 00:38:14.020
 Well, first of all, so to clarify, there's a nice Facebook in particular, but Facebook and Twitter and all of these social media outlets,

00:38:14.020 --> 00:38:29.480
 and also Google and YouTube, they've taken this idea that they are supposed to be suppressing or shutting down the sharing of what they call misinformation.

00:38:30.560 --> 00:38:32.700
 How do they define misinformation?

00:38:33.520 --> 00:38:41.140
 What they have done is they have outsourced it to what are called independent fact checkers.

00:38:41.140 --> 00:38:51.620
 Now, if you look at how independent are they, a lot of them actually receive funding from Facebook and Twitter and Google.

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:56.820
 So, it's not really an independent thing.

00:38:56.820 --> 00:39:08.860
 So, there was an independent, one of the so-called independent fact checkers that Facebook approves of.

00:39:09.280 --> 00:39:19.360
 They did a piece, because people were sharing this article on social media, they did a fact check on this.

00:39:19.740 --> 00:39:22.620
 This is climate feedback, they did a fact check.

00:39:22.760 --> 00:39:24.520
 Yeah, let's be clear.

00:39:24.620 --> 00:39:37.160
 It's climate feedback, and they are a group of scientists, and not just scientists, that are wedded to the view that humans are causing, you know, the FCC view, which is humans are causing climate change.

00:39:37.780 --> 00:39:41.980
 So, they're the ones doing the fact check on your article questioning that.

00:39:41.980 --> 00:39:55.440
 Yeah, and so, they went and they issued a fact check saying that the Epoch Times article was false and misleading, or inaccurate, incorrect, and misleading.

00:39:56.160 --> 00:40:06.440
 And then, Facebook said that this is, therefore, false misinformation, and anybody that shared it would receive this notification that they couldn't do it.

00:40:06.580 --> 00:40:09.180
 And you were, so they were suppressing it that way.

00:40:09.480 --> 00:40:16.740
 As do others, this is still on, this fact check, an alleged fact check is still on their website as well, and it's still active.

00:40:17.580 --> 00:40:32.140
 So, we tried to find out, what do you do, because they are the, this is, Facebook are very clear, they're saying, oh, this isn't us, this is one of the independent fact checkers.

00:40:32.140 --> 00:40:37.800
 So, if you have a complaint, they're basically playing Pontius Pilate, we wash our hands.

00:40:38.200 --> 00:40:38.920
 Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:40:39.040 --> 00:40:45.660
 So, they will say, oh, you need to contact the fact checker in question.

00:40:45.820 --> 00:40:59.960
 So, we looked at it, and other than saying, this is, there's not, they have not made it easy to actually modify, say, oh, here's what you're doing.

00:40:59.960 --> 00:41:13.900
 So, we found out the organizers of it, and we decided to write an open letter to them, but also letting other people know about it, to see what's happening.

00:41:14.020 --> 00:41:34.620
 Because this is, we did our fact check, fact check, on their fact check, and we used the guidelines that Climate Feedback claims they are using.

00:41:34.620 --> 00:41:42.060
 So, Climate Feedback, it's part of a sister company of what's called Science Feedback.

00:41:42.200 --> 00:41:52.800
 So, Science Feedback also has one that they're doing on COVID, we won't get into that, but they have another medicine feedback, or medical feedback, and then, so they have this Climate Feedback.

00:41:53.300 --> 00:42:02.120
 So, the science feedback, the science feedback website lists their criteria for checking, fact checking.

00:42:03.080 --> 00:42:07.200
 There were 14 potential forms of misinformation.

00:42:07.980 --> 00:42:17.120
 We went, and we looked through what their fact check on the Epoch Times article was.

00:42:17.480 --> 00:42:24.380
 They made 11 of the 14 errors that count as misinformation.

00:42:25.200 --> 00:42:27.760
 Epoch Times article made zero.

00:42:29.140 --> 00:42:34.780
 So, using their standards, they, the fact check.

00:42:35.020 --> 00:42:36.480
 And have they responded to this?

00:42:37.060 --> 00:42:38.700
 You pointed this out to them, open letter.

00:42:39.340 --> 00:42:42.560
 We haven't heard back from them yet, and it still seems to be on the website.

00:42:43.000 --> 00:43:00.240
 But, like, I will point out to you this, so, to your listeners, that I hope that they actually have the sense to actually correct it.

00:43:00.560 --> 00:43:01.780
 It is actually simple.

00:43:01.780 --> 00:43:16.260
 So, if their genuine goal is to be fact checking and to be reducing the spread of misinformation, using their criteria, their fact check is incorrect.

00:43:16.660 --> 00:43:21.120
 It's false and misleading, and it fails 11 of their 14 requirements.

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:23.760
 So, they can update that.

00:43:24.020 --> 00:43:28.400
 So, if they have integrity and they're intellectually honest, they'll update that, fix the problem.

00:43:28.660 --> 00:43:32.500
 If they don't, which my suspicion is.

00:43:32.620 --> 00:43:40.760
 Well, they can update it and say that the Epoch Times article was correct, was giving valid reporting.

00:43:41.200 --> 00:43:42.300
 We made a mistake, yeah.

00:43:42.300 --> 00:43:53.440
 Yeah, and to actually say, say the opposite, say, actually, the Epoch Times does, is correct and accurate, which it is.

00:43:54.100 --> 00:44:13.400
 And, by the way, if readers, I'd encourage your listeners to actually look up the Epoch Times article and read it, because the journalist, Alex Newman, in very, like, I'm, I was quite impressed.

00:44:13.620 --> 00:44:16.420
 He went with balanced reporting.

00:44:16.420 --> 00:44:32.440
 So, there's a lot of journalists now are taking what's this thing, narrative-driven journalism, where they will go, they decide what the story is and that the narrative is that their editors want and that they want.

00:44:32.660 --> 00:44:39.560
 And then they go and decide, right, if it agrees with the narrative, then we will report it.

00:44:39.700 --> 00:44:49.500
 If it disagrees with the narrative, then we either don't report it or else we misrepresent it to sound like it's problematic.

00:44:50.800 --> 00:44:56.280
 Now, the old style of journalism is called balanced reporting.

00:44:56.820 --> 00:45:09.880
 So, what you do with balanced reporting is you actually, you interview the relevant perspectives that are of interest to the viewership.

00:45:10.000 --> 00:45:13.220
 You go and you report, you say, these are the different perspectives.

00:45:13.620 --> 00:45:15.100
 These are the relevant ones.

00:45:15.980 --> 00:45:24.980
 And then you present them both or multiple to your viewers or your readers, and you let them decide.

00:45:24.980 --> 00:45:34.560
 The old, that classical approach to journalism, journalists are not supposed to be telling the readers what they should think.

00:45:35.140 --> 00:45:39.900
 Journalists, newsprint journalists have, many of them, I won't say all of them,

00:45:40.000 --> 00:46:00.520
 many of them, have become tantamount to the host of news talk shows on CNN, MSNBC, and even some Fox talk shows,

00:46:00.520 --> 00:46:08.640
 where basically what the reporters are doing is giving their opinion of the day's events,

00:46:09.040 --> 00:46:11.760
 not reporting on the day's events.

00:46:12.200 --> 00:46:19.080
 Oh, and there's a selection in what the day's events can be reported on.

00:46:19.680 --> 00:46:20.360
 Oh, yeah, that's right.

00:46:20.460 --> 00:46:20.840
 No, there is.

00:46:21.620 --> 00:46:29.080
 That's editorial content, you know, the background editorializing in how they choose what news they report on.

00:46:29.080 --> 00:46:32.400
 So, that's what I call narrative-driven journalism.

00:46:33.300 --> 00:46:37.640
 And it's, it's, it's, it's, you have a narrative and you promote stuff.

00:46:37.900 --> 00:46:38.620
 Now, so.

00:46:39.300 --> 00:46:41.660
 Wait, we're going, we're going a little long here.

00:46:42.120 --> 00:46:42.420
 Okay.

00:46:42.720 --> 00:46:46.920
 We got a, we got a, we got a, I want to, I want to close.

00:46:47.280 --> 00:46:47.560
 Okay.

