 And our experience of it, as I said, this was not, if, if this is about a genuine fact check, then the solution is simple.

00:47:36.580 --> 00:47:49.620
 The website just looks, they say, wait, here's 11 of the 14 forms of misinformation that we have warned against is being used by one of our fact checkers.

00:47:49.800 --> 00:47:52.160
 And the original article had zero.

00:47:52.160 --> 00:48:04.320
 So actually this original article, instead of being labeled false or, you know, incorrect and misleading, we should reverse that to correct and accurate.

00:48:04.860 --> 00:48:14.140
 And if they, if, if the goal of that website is genuine fact checking, then that's their solution.

00:48:14.140 --> 00:48:21.840
 If their goal is a narrative check, well, then they might continue doing what they're doing.

00:48:22.160 --> 00:48:31.080
 But, um, I, but, but that has nothing to do with facts and, and, and if they correct themselves, then it might, it might be important.

00:48:31.420 --> 00:48:35.080
 So we wrote the, in our open letter, we've encouraged them.

00:48:35.160 --> 00:48:37.240
 We've said, look, this is a way to do it.

00:48:37.320 --> 00:48:44.220
 If they are concerned, if they are genuinely concerned about fact checking, then that's the simple solution.

00:48:44.220 --> 00:48:57.200
 Another point just to do is that in the old days, balanced reporting, uh, the journalists and the editors were doing the fact checking.

00:48:58.040 --> 00:49:01.720
 So that was, that this was part of your job.

00:49:01.720 --> 00:49:16.120
 So this new idea that, uh, that the journalists and editors, uh, and, and reporters are, don't know any to care about fact checking.

00:49:16.120 --> 00:49:28.760
 Well, that's, well, this is, look, we live in an age where it's published rumor and innuendo now on the front page and then apologize later, uh, when you're, when you're wrong or not.

00:49:28.820 --> 00:49:33.800
 But if you do, when you apologize later, you don't pull the original story down.

00:49:33.900 --> 00:49:40.260
 You may have a little star and a little label at the bottom of, or more importantly, you publish on the back pages, your apology.

00:49:40.420 --> 00:49:40.860
 Yes.

00:49:40.980 --> 00:49:41.640
 Your correction.

00:49:41.800 --> 00:49:44.520
 It's never as prominent as the error itself.

00:49:45.160 --> 00:49:51.120
 Um, so if I just, I just the final thing, I guess, cause that we were talking about fact checking.

00:49:51.120 --> 00:50:02.260
 So that's something to bear in mind, uh, to look out for balanced reporting in journalism, uh, that, that if, is it narrative driven journalism or, or is it balanced reporting?

00:50:02.540 --> 00:50:10.100
 Just if it look out for yourself and you, I think a lot of your listeners will start to, to see.

00:50:10.420 --> 00:50:13.780
 Narrative driven journalism in terms of science.

00:50:13.780 --> 00:50:30.540
 Uh, if, if, if, if, if an organization is telling you that, uh, that scientific disagreement needs to be, it needs to be suppressed, then they're not interested in science.

00:50:30.540 --> 00:50:35.180
 People that scientists disagree all of the time with each other.

00:50:35.180 --> 00:50:46.060
 And even with themselves, that's why we do science because we're curious, we're trying to understand why do these different, why, why is this not clear?

00:50:46.220 --> 00:50:48.780
 Why does this disagree disagree with my expectations?

00:50:48.940 --> 00:51:02.400
 So as soon as you have people saying science, uh, there is a, we should, you should just go with one thing, one unified set of opinions on a particular issue.

00:51:02.520 --> 00:51:04.160
 Well, that is in science.

00:51:04.640 --> 00:51:05.000
 Yeah.

00:51:05.220 --> 00:51:06.900
 And well, that's an important point.

00:51:07.120 --> 00:51:11.260
 It may be the most important point, um, of many that you've made.

